findings.body:
**Issue:** `child_process.exec` runs the command through a shell. `src` and `filter` are interpolated into that shell string with no sanitization or allowlist. Any caller-controlled value becomes remote code execution on the host — e.g. `filter` set to `Lanczos; rm -rf /` or `src` set to a value that closes the double quote and appends shell metacharacters.

**Failure mode:** Power-user composed filter expressions (per the PR description) are exactly the attack surface: metacharacters (`;`, `|`, `$()`, backticks, quotes) are interpreted by the shell before `convert` sees them. Quoting `"${src}"` does not help once `src` contains a double quote.

**Fix:** Keep `execFile` (no shell) and pass arguments as an argv array. Validate `filter` against ImageMagick known filter names before use:

```ts
import { execFile } from "child_process";

const ALLOWED_FILTERS = new Set(["Lanczos", "Mitchell", "Catrom", "Cubic", "Triangle", "Point"]);

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  if (!ALLOWED_FILTERS.has(filter)) {
    return Promise.reject(new Error(`unsupported filter: ${filter}`));
  }
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) =>
      e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png")
    );
  });
}
```

A shell is not required for `-filter`; it is a single argv token.
